<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirtipur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kirtipur is an old Newar town on a ridge south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of Gorkha failed to take it in two assaults before capturing it after a long siege in the 1760s, punishing its defenders by cutting off their noses. Its fall opened the way to his conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in 1768.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirtipur.jpg" alt="Kirtipur" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Etter Studio · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirtipur,_1950_-_1955.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kirtipur, in Nepal Bhasa Kipoo, is a municipality and an ancient city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, 5 km south-west of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>. It is one of the five dense municipalities of the valley, with Kathmandu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> and Madhyapur Thimi. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> kirti, glory, and pur, city, and also from the Kirat kingdom of Yalamber's dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newars</a> are the natives of Kirtipur, which remains a centre of Newar culture. Its southern part is surrounded by Champa Devi Hill, to which people around the valley hike on the Nepali New Year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kirtipur's history dates from 1099, and it was part of the territory of Lalitpur when the Gorkhali king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in the 18th century. In 1767 Kirtipur was annexed to the Gorkhali kingdom after the Battle of Kirtipur. He took the town on his third attempt, after entering it by trickery, and then cut off the noses of the people, men and women, over 13 years of age.</p><p>In the 2006 mass uprising that overthrew the powers of the king, Kirtipur was the site of a peaceful demonstration. Because of its history under the Shah dynasty, whose modern founder conquered the city, it is considered an anti-monarchy city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> tentative site in 2008. Its municipality had 67,171 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and 81,578 at the 2021 census. Among its temples, the three-storey Bagh Bhairab temple, probably built in the 16th century, is dedicated to the tiger form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, regarded as the guardian of Kirtipur. The Uma Maheshwor temple stands at the highest point of the town, 1414 metres, and was built in 1655 by Rautra Vishwanath Babu, a son of a king. The Chilancho Stupa carries an inscription of Nepal Samvat 635. Tribhuvan University, the oldest and largest university in Nepal, is located in Kirtipur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kirtipur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After two failed assaults he took the hill town of <b>Kirtipur</b> in 1766, and in September 1768 his troops entered Kathmandu during the festival of Indra Jatra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prithvi Narayan Shah, who took the throne in 1743, blockaded the Kathmandu Valley's trade, took the Kingdom of Makwanpur in 1762 and <b>Kirtipur</b> in 1767, and armed his soldiers with muskets.</blockquote><a href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
